# Harness core v2

The harness's runtime, rebuilt in the shape of [HarnessPro](https://github.com/pzs19/HarnessPro)
(commit `6fd2d4d`, 2026-10-28): a short always-loaded contract, a `policy.json` with Lite,
Medium and Heavy profiles, and one standard-library hook. Everything else the harness knows lives
in modules that a prompt loads only when it needs them. Decision record: `docs/adr/0017-core-v2.md`.

| File | What it is |
|---|---|
| `instructions.md` | The contract. Installed as the always-loaded rule file of every agent (`~/.claude/rules/harness-runtime.md`, Codex/Copilot instructions; OMP gets it at session start). |
| `policy.json` | Profiles, the prompt classifier, heavy paths, guard prefilters, output limits and the module table. Tune behaviour here, not in code. |
| `hook.py` | The wired entry: loads `runtime.py` (bytecode-cached) and falls back to the last tested copy when it breaks (Entry and last-good copy below). Small and stable; replace it only by an atomic rename. |
| `runtime.py` | The one hook's logic. Standard library only; guard modules under `scripts/` are loaded by path only when a command or edit matches their prefilter. |
| `install.py` | `apply`, `status`, `env`. |
| `measure.py` | Median latency and injected bytes per event; `--score` rates the classifiers on a labelled set. |

## Events

| Event | Claude | Codex | Job |
|---|---|---|---|
| UserPromptSubmit | yes | yes | Guess the profile from the words of the prompt at once and start a background second opinion from the client's small model, name up to three modules plus those the session's project calls for (`context_modules`: a web project loads `web` on "ok, go" too, its `package.json` read once per session and directory), and on a session's first prompt add the marker `HARNESS_ACTIVE:core-v2:<hash>` and the user's learned preferences (`user-memory.md`, capped at 1,600 characters). Show the few items of the user's own apps the prompt is about (Recall below). A lite prompt after the first injects nothing else. URLs are not read as the user's words (a host like `x.harness-cards.pages.dev` made a question heavy). A client's one-shot helper prompt (Orca's "Name this chat ...", 63 a day) gets nothing and starts no model call; a relayed message (Stop hook feedback, another session's message) continues the task like a task notification. |
| PreToolUse | yes | yes | Safety rails, plus two Claude Agent checks: unsafe deletion targets (`scripts/permission_judge.py` `target_rail`), the shell guard (`scripts/shell_guard.py`), the money freeze (`scripts/money_policy.py`), unproven model-config edits (`scripts/model_preflight.py`), user-only files (`own-part.md`, `explorations.md`), one refusal of a whole-file read of a Markdown file over 40 KB, plan mode only when the user asked for a plan; for Claude's Agent tool, a refusal of Jobs review round 6 when the brief's `jobs-review.json` holds 5 rounds and the latest is not satisfied, and a note pointing a fresh verifier at the session's acceptor (resume it with SendMessage); for Claude's SendMessage to that acceptor asking for a verdict, a diff-only note at round 2 and a refusal of round 4 (7, 10, ...) until the request carries `Approach re-evaluated:` or the user asked for more rounds (review must converge: 29% of judged acceptors ran 3+ rounds, 2026-10-28; rounds per acceptor live in the session state, observed history in `review_ledger.py stats`); the first edit of a front-end file brings the `web` module once to a Claude session that has not had it (`context_modules`; harness and temp-folder pages excepted); and the move to heavy when an edit touches a heavy path or a command changes the machine or agent setup (`escalate_commands`). |
| PostToolUse | yes | yes | Apply the second opinion when it has arrived (it never lowers a profile an edit or command raised); count tool calls per prompt; remember the acceptors Claude spawns (id, description, prompt number); at the profile's economy and strict counts, tell the agent to economise or converge. After an edit, queue the checks the edited path maps to (Edit-time checks below) and report any that failed. Codex only: a shell output over the profile's limit is shortened to a head/tail view and kept in full under `<state>/core/raw/<session>/`. |
| PermissionRequest | yes | — | Claude stops for a command it finds dangerous; the independent reviewer (`permission_judge.verdict`) answers instead of the user. Fires only then, so it costs nothing on ordinary calls. A command the model could never allow (not every target a literal scratch path) is denied at once without the model call (22 decisions on 2026-10-29, none allowed). |

Copilot CLI and OMP keep their wiring (`scripts/agent_wiring.py`); `scripts/agent-hook.py` sends
their UserPromptSubmit and PreToolUse to this hook when the mode is v2.

## Entry and last-good copy

`core/hook.py` is what every client runs; `core/runtime.py` is the logic (docs/adr/0028). The runtime
is edited in place, and on 2026-10-29 that put 32 hook errors into live sessions in 18 hours (a policy
key the hook and policy disagreed about, invalid JSON, a NameError, a SyntaxError), each failing open
silently. Now:

- When `runtime.py` cannot load or raises, `<state>/core/last-good/runtime.py` with its own
  `policy.json` answers, and the agent is told once per session and error (kept in
  `<state>/core/notices/`, apart from the session state). With no last-good copy the call fails open
  with that notice; a permission request is denied.
- Only tested bytes reach last-good: the `promote` row of `edit_checks` (core-v2) reads `runtime.py`
  and `policy.json` before `test_core_v2` and writes them after a pass only if they are unchanged
  (`LastGood` rows in `events.jsonl`). A session's first prompt starts the same test in the background,
  once per source version, when last-good does not hold the current source (a pull on another machine).
- Not covered: an error in `hook.py` itself (no fallback; `python-syntax` checks it at edit time) and the
  guard modules under `scripts/`, which load from the working tree and fail open one by one.
- A missing `hook.py` blocks every tool call (Python exits 2, a blocking hook error in Claude Code), so
  the file is replaced only by writing a temporary file and renaming it over the old one.

Loading the runtime as a module lets Python reuse its compiled bytecode instead of compiling the whole
file on every event: 5 to 7.5 ms less per event (paired runs below).

Adopted from HarnessPro on 2026-10-29:

- **Confidence override.** The second opinion ends with `sure` or `unsure`; an unsure label in
  `second_opinion.unsure_raise` (only `lite`) is raised one step, never further, and never lowers
  anything. With the rubric's new line that a change is lite only when a direct look confirms it,
  the 100 labelled prompts of `evals/core-v2/` scored 94/100 in two runs against 92/100 for the old
  rubric, with no heavy task called lite.
- **Requirement recheck at each stage.** The economy and convergence reminders ask which
  requirements work end to end, fail or lack real evidence (`LEDGER_CHECK` in `hook.py`; ProgramBench's
  semantic evidence checkpoint).
- **Diff hygiene.** `diff-hygiene` in `edit_checks` runs `git diff --check` on each edited tracked file.
- **Verification traps.** The `verify` module carries five checks from SWE-bench Pro's
  `contract-verification.md` (throwaway state, every backend, config shape and exact IDs, a real
  fixture per branch, residue). Local incidents match them (`lessons.md`: scene-key case,
  snake/camel fields, fixtures that never crossed a resize cap, 195 untracked files in a deliverable).
  It fires on testing words only, so a typo fix or a domain prompt keeps its module slot.

- **The evolution loop's Stage 3** (fixed synthesized tests, hermetic snapshot runs, Pareto and J_m
  selection) lives in retrospection, not in the runtime: `scripts/harness_pareto.py`, ADR 0026.

Not adopted from HarnessPro, with the evidence:

- **RTK command rewriting.** In 29,793 Bash calls from 400 recent Claude sessions (2026-10-29),
  git and test commands produced about 3.5% of output characters, while `sed`, `cat`, `grep` and
  `ls` produced about half. RTK 1.1.0 cut a sample of real git and `ls` commands only to 88% of
  their size, and dropped about 95 hunk lines from `git diff` behind a `--no-compact` hint.

- **A model classifier in the prompt's path.** HarnessPro waits for the model; here it would add
  about 2 s to every prompt. The word rules answer in milliseconds and the client's Opus model
  answers in the background (`second_opinion` in `policy.json`, about 2 s); its profile replaces the
  guess at the next tool call. Blind real prompts (2026-10-28, `evals/core-v2/`): word rules 60/80,
  Haiku about 75%, Opus 74/80 (17/20 on the last fully held-out set), no heavy task called lite;
  two independent labellers agreed on 59/60. Score any labelled set with
  `python core/measure.py --score <file.jsonl>`.
- **Provisional heavy guidance until the second opinion arrives** (considered 2026-10-29). Of 51
  heavy guesses the second opinion later lowered, the correction arrived after the first tool call in
  43 and after the second in 7; only 1 had an edit or Agent spawn before it. Showing heavy duties late
  would cost real heavy tasks more than it saves; the URL fix removed the most common wrong guess instead.
- **A hard tool budget (`finalize` deny).** Personal tasks have no fixed size; stages warn, nothing denies.
- **Output compaction on Claude.** Claude Code 2.1.310 ignores `updatedToolOutput` for Bash (live
  check 2026-10-28: the model saw all 15,088 characters) and already saves an output over 30 KB to a
  file with a preview. Codex honours the replacement, so Codex gets it.
- **SessionStart and Stop hooks.** The contract is the rule file, so nothing needs injecting at
  start; the Stop gate was the main source of friction (it blocked the plain reply "Fixed the typo.").
  Verification is the acceptor's job, as the contract says.

## Profiles

| Profile | Chosen when | Guidance | Economy / converge at |
|---|---|---|---|
| lite | a question, lookup, typo or short prompt | do it directly plus the reversible extras its outcome plainly needs, listed under `Also done:`; check what changed | 12 / 24 tool calls |
| medium | an ordinary change (the default) | contract reading, aim at the outcome with reversible extras reported, the doer verifies by real use with cited evidence; no acceptor | 30 / 60 |
| heavy | rules, hooks, money, secrets, deploys, deletion of data, machine installs, all-project work; or an edit to a heavy path, a new harness module, a new dependency or install script | acceptor criteria led by a build verdict before the first edit, verified steps, the acceptor's verdict with `Not covered:`, `You answer for:` when it ships | 60 / 120 |

The instant guess (word rules, URLs removed first): an always-heavy pattern (force push, refunds, API
keys) wins; a typo is lite; a question about a heavy topic without a request to act is lite; a standing
preference that is not a question ("以后每次 ...", "from now on") is heavy; then heavy, then an action
verb or a bug report (medium), then a question or lookup (lite), then length. On the 112 labelled
prompts of `evals/core-v2/` the word rules score 91 against 84 before (2026-10-29), no set lower. The small model's answer then
replaces it; an edit to a heavy path or a machine-changing command raises it to heavy whatever the
classifiers said. `HARNESS_CORE_SECOND_OPINION=0` turns the model off (tests and probes).

## Review duties

The five places a person stays in the loop (docs/adr/0021), and what carries each:

| Duty | Carried by |
|---|---|
| Right thing to build | contract Work 2; the heavy acceptor leads with a build verdict (build / build smaller / drop); unasked extras pass it too |
| Dangerous code | `escalate_paths`: updater scripts, billing paths in any repo, `Dockerfile*`, workflows, deploy configs, `.env`, `money/` |
| Architecture | `escalate_content`: a new module under the harness's `scripts/`, `core/`, `mcp/`; an edit that adds a dependency or install-time script (`scripts/change_risk.py` compares the manifest before and after) |
| Proofs | a `PASS` carries `Not covered:`; an acceptor verdict line reading PASS without it (foreground Agent result or task notification; a SendMessage result carries no reply text) gets "not acceptance yet"; after an edit, each path no automatic check sees is named once |
| Responsibility | a heavy change that ships ends with `You answer for:` (what changed, what is not covered, how to roll it back); no gate |

When Claude spawns an acceptor whose brief does not mention `Not covered`, the hook adds a note listing
these duties (`subagents.brief_note`).

## Edit-time checks

Review here asks what could go wrong with a change and which check catches it, and runs that check
while the code is written, like a spellcheck, instead of only at the end. `policy.json`
`edit_checks.checks` is the table: each row has a path pattern, the risk in words, and the cheapest
command that sees it.

| Row | Paths | Check |
|---|---|---|
| python-syntax | any `*.py` | parse it (about 70 ms) |
| json-parse | any `*.json` except JSONC (tsconfig, `.vscode`) | parse it |
| diff-hygiene | any file git tracks, except Markdown (its line breaks are trailing spaces) | `git diff --check`: new whitespace errors only, CRLF allowed; untracked files and files outside a repository pass. `coverage: false`: it never counts as seeing a file's risk, so "No automatic check sees" still names files no other row covers |
| core-v2 | `core/hook.py`, `runtime.py`, `policy.json`, `instructions.md`, `install.py`, `measure.py` | `test_core_v2` (about 30 s); a pass promotes the last-good copy |
| rule-conflicts | harness `*.md`, `core/policy.json`, `core/hook.py`, `scripts/rule_conflicts.py` | `test_rule_conflicts` (about 3 s) |
| portable-paths | harness files that can carry a path | `test_portable_paths` (under 1 s) |
| architecture | harness `*.py` modules | `test_architecture` (about 2.5 s) |
| module-tests, edited-test | `scripts/x.py` with a `scripts/tests/test_x.py`; a test module itself | that test module |
| money-deploy-secrets | `money/`, deploy configs, workflows, `.env`, `credentials.py` | none: the effect is outside the repo, so the task's acceptor or the user reviews it (`escalate_paths` already raise these edits to heavy) |

How it runs: PostToolUse on an edit tool (`edit_tools`) adds the matching jobs to the session's
queue, each due `debounce_s` (4 s) after the latest edit that touched it, so a burst of edits runs
each check once, after the burst. If no runner has beaten its heartbeat lately, the hook starts one
background `hook.py --run-checks <session>` with `CREATE_NO_WINDOW`; it runs due jobs one at a time
(an edit during a run queues one rerun after it), kills a job at `timeout_s` (180 s), and exits when
the queue is empty. A pass says nothing. A failure or timeout is reported once, at the next tool
call, with the check, the files, the command, the output's tail and the full output's path
(`<state>/core/checks/<session>/<check>.txt`); the first pass after a failure says "passes again".
The full suite (`scripts/run-tests.py`) stays the end check. A longer debounce for core-v2 was
considered on 2026-10-29 and dropped: its failures that day were runs minutes apart that really failed,
not one change split over two edits.

A check's process carries `HARNESS_CORE_EDIT_CHECKS=0` (and no second opinion), so a test that
drives this hook with edit payloads never starts a further runner; the tests set it too, and
`HARNESS_CORE_EDIT_CHECKS_DEBOUNCE` shortens the debounce for them. Every run is one `EditCheck` row
in `<state>/core/events.jsonl` (check, files, start, ms, pass/fail/timeout). After a week,
`python core/measure.py --checks` shows per check how often it ran, failed and timed out; a row that
never fails is a candidate for removal (docs/adr/0004).

Cost (paired runs, 15 each, 2026-10-28): the first edit of a burst pays the runner's spawn, about
20 ms (66 to 87 ms median); an edit while the runner is alive and every other event are within
3 ms of the hook before this change.

## Modules

`policy.json` `modules`: web, seo, money, credentials, browser, deploy, fleet, board, report, lessons,
harness, ui-taste, verify. Each has trigger patterns and one line naming what to read or run. The hook
shows at most three per prompt, each once per session. The module's files and scripts stay where
they are; only their loading changed. Removing a module entry removes its hint and nothing else.

## Recall

The agent should not wait to be told to look at the user's mail and chats (user 2026-10-21 "agent 必须自己主动
收集", 2026-10-29; docs/adr/0030). On every prompt the hook ranks the attention snapshot
(`<state>/context/attention.json`, written by `scripts/attention.py`) against the prompt with
`scripts/attention_recall.py` and shows at most three items it has not shown in this session, each one
line with its source, time and sender, under a header that marks them as untrusted data written by
others. A rare shared term (inverse document frequency over the snapshot) scores, and an item needs at
least one distinctive shared term: a word with a digit (a build id), a word the prompt writes with a
capital (APEX, a person's name; the first word only before Chinese text), or one of the user's project
names (the folders beside the harness checkout). Never a platform name (Google, Windows, Node), a long
all-caps word (WITHOUT), a bare number, or a word inside a path, URL or image tag. A capitalised word
names an item only where the item writes it the same way, and in a prompt over 600 characters (a brief,
whose headings and tables capitalise ordinary words) only ids and project names count. Ordinary lower-case words and Chinese
bigrams only add to a score, so "review the design doc", "写个测试用例" and other generic prompts get nothing. Items that look like
credentials are left out whole. The audit row keeps only the count.

Freshness: when the snapshot is older than `recall.refresh_s` (an hour), a prompt starts one
`attention.py --quiet` in the background (no window; no feeds, which open a browser tab), at most once an
hour across sessions; the file is written beside and renamed over, so a reader never sees half of it.
The agent searches further with `python <harness>/scripts/attention_recall.py "<terms>"` (or
`attention.py recall`). `policy.json` `recall` tunes it; `HARNESS_CORE_ATTENTION=0` turns it off.
Cost (paired runs, 21 each, three pairs, 2026-10-29, `evals/core-v2/measure-recall-20261029.json`): a prompt
that matches nothing pays 0 to 20 ms (median about 5 ms) over the live 54 KB snapshot; a prompt that shows
items pays about 20 ms more, mostly the credential screens and `transcripts`, which load only then; tool
events are unchanged. Five acceptor rounds tuned this against made-up and real prompts; with the final rule 0 of 30 generic and
technical prompts recall anything on the live snapshot, and about 10% of 120 real past prompts do, almost
all on the user's own project names.

Not covered: the phone and other devices (attention.md names each gap), a chat's messages beyond the last
eight the Teams cache holds, and meaning without shared words ("今天有什么会" finds no meeting: the agent
runs the CLI or reads attention.md), a name typed in lower case or opening an English sentence ("bowen",
"Bowen wants ..."), and a hung refresh, which is never killed (the next starts an hour later).

## Install and check

    python <harness>/core/install.py apply     # rule file = instructions.md, hooks -> core/hook.py,
                                               # on the detected agents (--clients names them instead)
    python <harness>/core/install.py status    # detected agents and how, entry counts, contract per agent
                                               # (current / stale / missing; OMP via its SessionStart hook;
                                               # not installed: not detected, e.g. Hermes on this machine)
    python <harness>/scripts/harness-hook-health.py   # wiring, Codex trust and behaviour probes, every agent
    python <harness>/core/install.py env       # create or upgrade the harness's own uv environment

The harness runs on its own uv environment, never the system Python (user 2026-10-28):
`<state>/venv`, with a uv-managed CPython 3.13 in `<state>/python` and `requirements-bootstrap.txt`.
`apply` started from a Python older than 3.11 re-runs itself inside it. Claude and
Codex hooks run that venv's base interpreter (a Windows venv `python.exe` is a launcher that costs
about 10 ms per hook event; `core/hook.py` needs only the standard library); Copilot, OMP, the rule
installer and the scheduled jobs (`\Harness\*`) run the venv's own `python.exe` / `pythonw.exe`.

Which agents get set up is detected, not fixed (docs/adr/0029): `scripts/harnesslib/agents.py`
`REGISTRY` lists each agent's homes (environment overrides such as `CODEX_HOME` first), executables,
hook adapter and contract delivery; `detect()` keeps the ones whose home exists or, on the real
profile, whose executable is on PATH. Nothing is created for an absent agent. Agents without hooks
here (opencode, Gemini CLI) get the contract as a `harness-managed:contract` block in their global
instructions file, the user's text around it unchanged. Another agent is one registry row.
An agent installed later is set up by itself: a session's first prompt starts
`python <harness>/core/install.py adopt` in the background at most hourly, which applies to agents
detected since `<state>/core/agents-seen.json` was written and logs each one to
`<state>/core/adopt.log` (`HARNESS_CORE_ADOPT=0` turns it off).

Every edit of an agent's config is backed up under `<state>/core/backups`. Only the harness's own
entries are touched; entries of the deleted legacy runtime are dropped, and Orca's and the user's
entries stay byte for byte. `apply` also moves the legacy bridge scripts (`~/.claude/hooks/harness-*.py`,
`~/.codex/hooks/harness-*.py`) into `<state>/core/backups/bridges-<time>/`.

## The legacy runtime (deleted 2026-10-29)

The legacy runtime (`scripts/runtime-hook.py` and what it composed: the Stop gates, next-action
checkpoints, lesson injection, the task router, the SessionStart context pack, the subagent hooks) and
the tools that only measured it (the paired replay, the guard bench, the lesson standing loop) were
deleted on 2026-10-29 (docs/adr/0024). Restore point: git tag `legacy-runtime-final`. The scheduled
jobs (outcomes, repo sync, money read, MCP sync, Orca watchdog, digests) are separate processes and
unchanged. A RULES.md rule whose only enforcer was a legacy Stop gate reads "Instruction only".

## Measurements

2026-10-29, paired runs (25 each, alternating, same interpreter; `evals/core-v2/measure-optimise-20261029.json`),
before and after the entry/runtime split:

| | Before | After |
|---|---|---|
| UserPromptSubmit, lite | 59.0 ms | 54.5 ms |
| PreToolUse Read / Bash | 53.1 / 59.4 ms | 46.9 / 52.1 ms |
| PostToolUse Bash | 57.0 ms | 49.5 ms |
| Always-loaded rule file | 6,303 B | 6,303 B (`test_core_v2` caps it at 6,600 B; growing past it means cutting first, which is the user's call) |

2026-10-28, this machine, `core/measure.py`, median of 5-7 runs:

| | Legacy (live state) | Core v2 |
|---|---|---|
| Session start | 616 ms, 4,590 B injected (12.5 s on a cold state dir) | no hook |
| First prompt | 364 ms, 842 B | 69 ms, 1,959 B (marker + memory) |
| Each later lite prompt | 376 ms, 842 B | 74 ms, 0 B |
| PreToolUse Bash / Read | 192 / 104 ms | 65 / 57 ms |
| PostToolUse | not installed | 66 ms |
| Stop on "Fixed the typo." | 368 ms, blocks | no hook, no block |
| Harness hook entries Claude / Codex | 9 / 6 | 4 / 3 |
| Always-loaded rule file | 2,522 B, which told the agent to read `runtime-policy.md` and `RULES.md` sections | 3,894 B, self-contained |

Raw numbers: `evals/core-v2/measure-*.json`. Acceptance criteria and verdict: `evals/core-v2/`.

