# Brief: Language Games

Build a Next.js web app of small games that teach **English** (to non-native learners, intermediate,
e.g. Chinese speakers) **through Ludwig Wittgenstein's philosophy** — and teach the philosophy
through the English. Both must be learned by *playing*, not by reading quizzes.

Constraints from the owner:
- Next.js (App Router, TypeScript), Tailwind, shadcn/ui. No login, no backend required for v1;
  progress kept in localStorage. Analytics: PostHog only (optional, env-gated).
- UI language: English only. One clear core flow; each game playable in a few minutes, on phone and desktop.
- Visual identity will be produced with gpt-image-2 (Codex image generation): illustrations,
  backgrounds, game art. Plan which images are needed and the art direction.
- Must be genuinely fun and philosophically correct (cite the source passages: Tractatus,
  Philosophical Investigations §§, On Certainty, Blue Book, etc.).
- No ads, no dark patterns, no engagement hooks such as streak guilt.

Deliverable for this planning step: an independent plan in Markdown with
1. Game concepts (at least 6), each: the Wittgenstein idea (with § citation), the English skill it
   trains (vocab / grammar / pragmatics / pronunciation...), the core mechanic, one round example,
   why it is fun, and how it is scored.
2. Which 3-4 games make v1 and why; the information architecture and screens.
3. Content model (data schema for rounds) and how much content v1 needs.
4. Art direction and the list of images to generate with gpt-image-2 (with prompts).
5. Technical architecture, risks, and what would make this fail.
Be concrete and opinionated. Write your plan to the file named in your instructions only.
# Decisions: merging the three plans

Inputs: `plan-claude.md`, `plan-codex.md`, `plan-copilot.md` (each written from `BRIEF.md` alone), then
`critique-claude.md`, `critique-codex.md`, `critique-copilot.md`. "Source" says whose idea won; "Against"
records who disagreed and why it was overruled or deferred.

| # | Decision | Source | Against / note |
|---|----------|--------|----------------|
| D1 | Every round must change a shared situation (stones arrive, a window closes, a pupil's answers change); no round is a quiz with a red ✗. | Codex | — all three agree on the risk; Codex stated the test most sharply. |
| D2 | Five games in v1: **Slab!**, **Picture Theatre**, **The Café**, **Go On!**, **Family Threads**. | Claude (merge) | Codex wanted exactly three (Slab!, Café, Family) and to defer Picture Theatre as overlapping with Slab!. Kept: it is the only game carrying the *Tractatus* (TLP 2.1, 2.21, 4.01) and the early/late contrast, and it trains a different skill (prepositions and truth-checking) with a different verb (arrange/judge). Copilot wanted three plus a gated fourth. With 8 hours, five were built; each has tests proving every authored round is winnable. |
| D3 | Slab! has two roles: fetch (listen) then call (speak through tiles); a literal-minded assistant asks "Which one?" when a call is genuinely unclear, never when it is merely short. "Slab!" alone is a complete order (PI §§19–20). | All three | — |
| D4 | No timers, lives, streaks or leaderboards. | Codex, Copilot | Claude's plan had a countdown in Slab!; dropped. |
| D5 | **Score the communicative outcome; coach English form separately.** "Two pillar — there!" still builds the gate and earns a one-line note ("After *two*, English needs the plural"). | Copilot (coaching), Codex (fought for it) | Claude's first Slab! auto-pluralised tiles and so never practised the plural it claimed; fixed by offering singular and plural tiles. A note costs no mark in Slab!'s outcome. |
| D6 | Speech is optional: browser text-to-speech reads calls and replies; the text is always on screen. | Claude | Codex: no audio in v1. Overruled: the builders' game is a game of calls, TTS costs nothing, and nothing depends on hearing. |
| D7 | The Café pairs identical words in changed situations ("It's a bit cold in here" with the window open / with the cup empty; "Do you know what time it is?" asked by someone in a hurry / by the owner with the keys). "Ask first" is always free. | Codex | Copilot's relationship meter (*Would You Mind?*) rejected per Codex critique: it silently rewards the author's favourite wording. |
| D8 | Go On!: the pupil holds a few authored "ways of going on"; examples rule them out (§185). **Two** explanation cards: a loose one the pupil misreads (§198) and a precise one it can follow *only after* an example has shown what its key word means. | Copilot (mechanic), Codex (fairness fix) | Codex: a single vague Explain card "rigs the argument against explanation". Fixed as above; the source note says the hypothesis list is a toy model and that the point is practice, not scepticism (§§198–202, 217). |
| D9 | Family Threads checks claims about **these** cards ("Most of these games have a winner"), never about all games; blurred features (is chess amusing? §66) count neither way; no feature runs through every card (content test). Second session uses Wittgenstein's own tool-box (§11) and his ruler counterexample (§14). | Copilot (claims), Claude (chain), Codex (scope fix) | Codex caught that Copilot's "Most games have a winner" generalised from six cards. |
| D10 | Hinge/certainty game and Beetle in a Box deferred. | Codex, Copilot | Claude's "Must, Might, Can't" treated a hinge as a fourth confidence setting, which Codex showed misreads OC §§23–25, 96–99. |
| D11 | Copy says the games are *inspired by* Wittgenstein's investigations, and quotes §43 with its "though not for all". | Codex | Claude's plan said the later philosophy "is a theory of language learning" — an overclaim (PI §109). |
| D12 | Art: one paper-theatre style for everything, generated with gpt-image-2 through Codex; sprites drawn on flat magenta and keyed to alpha; backgrounds and sprites generated separately so art never decides a mark. | Codex (separation), Claude (style) | Three UI directions were mocked up with image2 and published for the owner to choose; B ("Puzzle Desk") was built, with C's § margin notes. |
| D13 | Progress and half-done rounds live in localStorage, versioned, merged across tabs, never overwriting a newer save; no account. | Codex | — |
| D15 | When every round is done the home page ends the journey with TLP 6.54 (throw away the ladder) paired with PI §133 (being able to stop) — no streaks or "come back tomorrow". | Claude (ladder), Copilot (§133 pairing) | — |
| D16 | Copilot's critique of Claude's plan: Slab! as timed recognition was A1 and failed WCAG 2.2.1; the shipped Slab! has no timer and a calling role with real form choices. Its OC §115 misattribution in the hinge concept is moot (concept deferred); the shipped §115 quote was checked against the German ("Das Spiel des Zweifelns selbst setzt schon die Gewißheit voraus"). | Copilot | — |
| D17 | Static export rejected: `next start`, security headers and the default image optimiser need a server build. | Copilot (critique of Codex §6) | Codex proposed static export. |
| D18 | Picture Theatre stays despite Copilot's objection that it teaches the early view that PI §23 corrects: its source notes say so explicitly ("Wittgenstein later criticised this picture view"), and the contrast with the Café is itself a lesson. | Claude | Copilot, Codex would defer it to v1.1. |
| D14 | Each game page serves its name, how-to-play, the English it trains and the passage in the HTML (SEO/GEO), plus JSON-LD. | Copilot (`/notes`), harness web defaults | Separate `/notes` articles deferred. |

## Deferred, with the reason
- Logical Space (Copilot): Picture Theatre already carries the Tractatus.
- Aspect Flip / Duck–Rabbit (Claude): Codex judged it a paraphrase quiz as specified; needs a consequential goal first.
- Look at the Use / glorp (Copilot): closest to a quiz; candidate for v1.1 if the use-test stays a test of use.
- Learner playtests (Codex's five-learner gate): not possible in this session; recommended to the owner before promotion.
# Acceptance criteria: Language Games

Written by the independent acceptor before the build (2026-10-31). The builder does not edit this file.

## 1. Build verdict

- Problem: intermediate English learners (e.g. Chinese speakers) drill vocabulary out of context;
  Wittgenstein's core idea, meaning is use inside a language game, is the best available theory of
  why that fails. Games where the learner uses English to get something done teach both at once.
- Cheaper way: a reading list or a quiz deck exists already and teaches neither by playing. No
  existing free product combines the two, so there is no cheaper equal.
- Verdict: **build smaller**. Ship 3-4 polished games (not 6+ thin ones) in one short core flow.
  The 8-hour floor buys depth and rounds of fixing, not more game count.
- Flaw that decides success: philosophy turned into trivia ("Which § says X?"). Every game must make
  the player *do* the idea (follow a rule, play a builder's game, find a family resemblance), or it fails.

## 2. Done-criteria and how each is checked

### A. Process (the user's "independent plans, critique, borrow")

| # | Criterion | Check |
|---|-----------|-------|
| A1 | Three independent plans exist: Claude, Codex CLI, Copilot CLI, each answering all 5 BRIEF sections | Files in `planning/` (e.g. `plan-claude.md`, `plan-codex.md`, `plan-copilot.md`); each has 6+ concepts with § citations |
| A2 | Plans were made independently | Run logs (`codex-run.log`, `copilot-run.log`) show each tool got only BRIEF.md, not another plan; file mtimes precede the critiques. Note: `copilot-run.log` was empty when this file was written |
| A3 | Each planner critiques the other two | Files `planning/critique-<author>-of-<target>.md` (or one per author covering both); each names at least 3 concrete flaws and 2 ideas worth borrowing, with section references |
| A4 | Critiques come from the real tools, not the builder impersonating them | Log of the CLI invocation (command, model, timestamp) for each Codex and Copilot critique |
| A5 | A merged decision record | `planning/DECISIONS.md`: for each choice (games, IA, content model, art, stack) which plan it came from, what was rejected and why |
| A6 | At least 8 hours of real work | First to last substantive commit/log entry spans >= 8 h; git log shows steady commits across the span, no gap > 90 min of idle waiting padded as work; a `logs/` worklog with timestamps |

### B. Product

| # | Criterion | Check |
|---|-----------|-------|
| B1 | Next.js App Router + TypeScript + Tailwind + shadcn/ui in D:/githubprojects/language-games | `package.json`, `components.json`, `app/` dir |
| B2 | `lint`, `typecheck` (`tsc --noEmit`), `build` all exit 0 | Acceptor runs them fresh; output captured |
| B3 | 3-4 games playable end to end in the production build | `next build && next start`; acceptor plays every game start -> finish -> result screen on desktop and phone viewport (Playwright, 390x844 and 1440x900); no console errors |
| B4 | Each game is a mechanic, not a quiz about the philosophy | Acceptor judges per game: the player performs the idea (e.g. builder's game PI §2, rule-following PI §§185-201, family resemblance PI §§66-67, beetle in a box PI §293). A multiple-choice "which philosopher said" round fails |
| B5 | Philosophy accurate with citations | Every in-game claim links to a source passage (work + § or proposition number); acceptor spot-checks at least 8 citations against the text (PI, Tractatus, On Certainty, Blue Book); zero misattributions; no invented quotes |
| B6 | English learning is real | Each game names its English target (vocab / grammar / pragmatics); feedback explains *why* an answer is wrong in English terms; content is CEFR B1-B2 level and natural; acceptor checks 10 random items for correct English |
| B7 | Enough content | At least ~20 rounds per v1 game, held as typed data (schema from the content model), not hard-coded JSX |
| B8 | Tests for game logic | Unit tests for scoring, round validation and state transitions of each game; `test` exits 0; a content test validates every round against the schema and that every round has a citation |
| B9 | gpt-image-2 art is actually used | Images generated through Codex image generation (log shows the command/model and prompts); files in `public/`; referenced by components; visible in the acceptor's screenshots; consistent art direction; optimised (`next/image`, sensible sizes) |
| B10 | Owner preferences | No login; progress and in-progress input survive reload (localStorage); one UI language (English); core flow short, mostly one screen; PostHog only and env-gated; no ads, streak guilt or dark patterns |
| B11 | Accessible | Keyboard playable; visible focus; alt text on art; contrast AA; axe (or Lighthouse a11y >= 95) with no serious violations; works with reduced motion |
| B12 | SEO/GEO basics | Per-page title/description, OG image, sitemap, robots, semantic headings, JSON-LD for the site |
| B13 | Secure | No secrets in repo; security headers (CSP or at least frame/content-type); `npm audit` no high/critical in production deps |
| B14 | Phone and desktop | No horizontal scroll at 360 px; touch targets >= 44 px; layout reviewed in screenshots at both sizes |
| B15 | jobs-review passed | A fresh reviewer used the running site on phone and desktop; its findings and the fixes are recorded; final round satisfied |
| B16 | README | How to run, test, build; where content and art live; how to add a round |

## 3. Status rule

PASS only when every row above passes with evidence captured in the verdict turn. Any failed row
is FAIL. A row with no reachable evidence after trying every route is UNVERIFIED with the exact blocker.

## 4. Not covered by these checks

- Whether real learners actually improve their English: no learner study, only expert judgment.
- Whether the games are fun for the target audience over time: the jobs-review and acceptor are
  proxies, not players from the target group.
- Full philosophical depth: spot checks of citations catch misquotes, not every subtle misreading.
- The 8-hour check proves elapsed, active work, not that every hour was well spent.
- Independence of the plans relies on logs; it cannot rule out a model's prior knowledge overlapping.
- Production hosting, real PostHog data, performance on low-end devices and slow networks, and
  browsers other than the Chromium used by Playwright.
- Licensing nuance of AI-generated art and of quoted translations (Anscombe's PI translation is
  under copyright; short quotes assumed fair use, not legally reviewed).

